What we have in Phase 1 is a "self-service onboarding + upload wizard" (the first-time experience) and an "analytics dashboard" (view conversations and stats). But a proper Web Asistan admin panel needs much more than that for the customer to manage their product on an ongoing basis:

**What's missing from the current plan:**

- **Content management panel** — upload new catalogs, replace old ones, see what's indexed, delete products, re-index after updates. A manufacturer updates their product catalog quarterly — they need a place to upload the new version and see it take effect.

- **Chatbot configuration** — customize widget colors to match their brand, set the welcome message, configure CTA buttons ("İletişim formu" vs "Bayinizi bulun"), set business hours for live chat handoff (if we add that later).

- **Training/correction interface** — when the chatbot gives a wrong answer, the manufacturer clicks thumbs-down and provides the correct answer. This feeds back into the knowledge base. Without this, the chatbot never improves.

- **Q****&****A override panel** — manufacturer adds manual Q&A pairs for common questions the catalog doesn't cover ("Kargo süresi ne kadar?" / "Bayilik başvurusu nasıl yapılır?").

- **Widget embed manager** — copy the embed code, see which domains the widget is deployed on, preview the widget.

- **User management** — if the Business tier has multiple users (admin, marketing manager, sales), they need role-based access to the admin panel.

This is probably 5–6 additional days of work in the Phase 1 sprint. I'd fold it into the "Self-service dashboard" task and expand that from 3 person-days to 8 person-days, renaming it to **"Admin Panel + Analytics Dashboard."** The upload wizard handles first-time onboarding; the admin panel handles everything after that.

Want me to update the implementation plan to reflect this, or shall we capture it as a note and move forward?

